  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now sitting in a wheelchair himself across from Patrick Malouda, Chef Barinov listens as Mikhail Jekovich piles on the drama—explaining that Viktor opened his own restaurant and created a signature menu, but terrible pain kept him from cooking the soup today.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Виктор решил открыть свой ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он создал своё авторское меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я же правильно всё рассказываю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, это моё авторское меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Вы просто настоящий герой, Виктор!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он молодец, работает каждый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Но он очень сильно устаёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Кому из нас звезда нужна?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, устаю, очень много работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Конечно, я вас прекрасно понимаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Из-за усталости у Виктора сильные боли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Поэтому сегодня готовил не он.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Виктор решил открыть свой ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor decided to open his own restaurant.' The meaning hinges on 'to decide' working together with 'restaurant' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он создал своё авторское меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He created his own signature menu.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'to create' places the action in the masculine past tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я же правильно всё рассказываю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Am I telling everything correctly?' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'but' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, это моё авторское меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, this is my signature menu.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'this' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Вы просто настоящий герой, Виктор!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are simply a true hero, Viktor!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'hero'. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он молодец, работает каждый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is great, he works every day.' The grammar packs 'he' and 'day' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Но он очень сильно устаёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But he gets very badly tired.' The meaning hinges on 'but' working together with 'gets tired' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Кому из нас звезда нужна?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which one of us needs the star?' Notice how Russian expresses 'to whom' and 'needed' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, устаю, очень много работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I get tired, there is a lot of work.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yes' and finishing on 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Конечно, я вас прекрасно понимаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course, I understand you perfectly.' The meaning hinges on 'of course' working together with 'understand' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Из-за усталости у Виктора сильные боли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because of fatigue Viktor has severe pains.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'because of' before landing on 'pains'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Поэтому сегодня готовил не он.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is why it was not he who cooked today.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'cooked' places the action in the masculine past tense. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
